build.reason:
No allowed facade can carry the recipe validly.

(1) asList() is reached by exactly 3 tests. What each test checks:
- testAddBooleanAttribute: one BooleanAttribute, size 1.
- parsesBooleanAttributes: [Attribute, BooleanAttribute, Attribute], size 3 and class per index.
- testByAttributeStarting: whether any key starts with data-; order and size are ignored; at most 2 attributes per element.

I ran full-suite mutants of asList in scratch:
- dropping the last element fails all 3 tests;
- dropping the first element fails all 3 tests;
- rotating by one, or moving the BooleanAttribute to the end, fails only parsesBooleanAttributes;
- reversing fails none, because the 3-element pattern is symmetric.

So the only single-trigger fault class is an order change for 3 or more elements. The original computation is a plain copy of LinkedHashMap values in insertion order, unwrapped and made unmodifiable. Its per-element core (add the entry's attribute) has no step whose one-line omission rotates or regroups the list. A rotation needs an artificial step (a circular start offset, a held-back first element, or a boolean/valued partition and merge) that the original does not perform and that an auditor would see as built for the fault. Copying attributes would let a class-based fault isolate the 'empty' attribute, but it breaks the documented view semantics (list elements are the map's own Attribute objects), so the correct configuration would no longer equal the original.

(2) getIgnoreCase / hasKeyIgnoreCase are on the path of Node.attr / Node.hasAttr, so faults in the value, not-found or first-match logic hit many tests. Full-suite mutants on case matching:
- case-sensitive equals: 5 tests each;
- regionMatches prefix match (both directions, both methods): 0 tests;
- lower-casing only the lookup key: 9 tests (get) and 6 (has);
- lower-casing only the stored key: 5 tests (get) and 2 (has).

The only single-test case pattern is a lookup key with upper-case letters that differ from the stored key (AttributesTest::html, getIgnoreCase("hEllo"); SelectorTest::caseInsensitive, hasKeyIgnoreCase("TITLE")). Isolating it needs an asymmetric fold (fold the stored character but not the requested one) plus a raw-equality shortcut. That is a sub-line change, not an omission, and it shows a T3(b)/(d) trace (one operand of the comparison normalised, the other not). No test exercises the first-match rule (two keys equal ignoring case).

Hence no quiet one-line omission in a hop-5 gold makes exactly one existing test newly fail while passing T1-T4 (BUILD_BRIEF step 7; the expected batch-8 'no single-trigger omission' outcome).

