The facade can be reached, but no fault in it can have the gold as its unique owner. Only testExtremeValues tells extreme values apart. Every other test reaching cumulativeProbability (testCumulativeProbabilities, testQuantiles, testMath280, testInverseCumulativeProbabilities, testConsistency, testSampling, PoissonDistributionTest::testNormalApproximateProbability) checks ordinary points with several (mean, sd) pairs. Dropping the mean subtraction, the sd scaling, the sqrt(2) or the 0.5(1+.) form would therefore break several tests and violate G5. So the omission has to be in extreme-value handling: the 40-sd tail decision, or a clamp or infinity case in its place. Drop it, and finite moderate x still evaluates correctly (Erf and Gamma converge up to 99 sd). The trigger then fails only at x = +/-Double.MAX_VALUE or +/-infinity, and only inside the special-function layer: Erf.erf squares the argument, the square overflows, and Gamma.regularizedGammaQ's continued fraction diverges with a ConvergenceException. Two one-line edits outside the chain fix that same failure, and each keeps every method on the path used as documented:
- Erf.erf saturating at |x| > 40 (the form later Commons Math releases adopted);
- Gamma.regularizedGammaP returning 1.0 for an infinite x, which is mathematically P(a, inf) = 1 and matches its existing overflow branch that returns 1.0.
These are competing repairs, so they reject any such design. I checked whether a reshaping could move the failure out of Erf and Gamma:
- clamping the erf argument, standardising first, splitting the cut into per-tail decisions (that would also be a T3(b) range-end trace), or calling Gamma directly from the gold all leave the failure inside Erf or Gamma;
- the gold's 0.5(1+erf) form always gives a correct value in [0,1] whenever Erf returns, so the only failure that does not come from the special functions would be a NaN made up in the gold's own arithmetic on infinite input. That would be an unnatural rewrite of the original computation, not a redistribution of it;
- a try/catch recovery, the shape of the real Math-60 code, can be recovered by any caller.
Moving the step into the facade would make the facade more than state gathering plus one call. The pre-filter at hop 1 (a tail check on x, mean, sd) remains as well. density has no single-trigger omission. This is the brief's step-7 case: no valid fault that only the gold owns exists for either allowed target.

